WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
there are new questions this morning  about a justice department effort to  tackle chinese spying mit professor gong  chin was among those arrested under the  so-called china initiative he faced up  to 20 years in prison accused of hiding  his ties with the chinese government in  a u.s government grant application but  last month the charges were suddenly  dropped in his first tv interview  professor chin chairs our chiefs tells  our chief investigative correspondent  jim axelrod that he believes the case  was racially and politically motivated  jim good morning good morning nate  donald trump's justice department  launched the china initiative in 2018 to  tackle a top national security priority  hunting down those responsible for  china's widespread theft of hundreds of  billions of dollars a year in american  trade secrets and intellectual property  we learned the department's search for  chinese spies spread beyond business and  government targets deep into the halls  of american academia where it ensnared  at least one innocent professor  who wasn't a spy at all  we thought we had achieved the american  dream until this nightmare happened gong  chen's nightmare began in january of  2020 a u.s citizen for more than two  decades the mit professor was returning  home from china when he his wife and his  two children were stopped by federal  agents at boston's logan airport what  were you thinking as this was happening  it was very uneasy they brought my  entire family to a separate area  and we sat there for a few hours agents  questioned him and demanded he'd turn  over his cell phone and computer then he  was let go and heard nothing until a  year later i was making coffee there  around 6 30 and i saw a group of people  chen whose family was a victim of mao's  regime 50 years before  suddenly had a group of federal agents  surrounding his house and what did they  say to you they said are you gang-chan i  said yes they said don't move my wife  was in her sleep and she told me she  heard the man yield police police she  saw that she was in a dream she thought  she was dreaming yeah they were going to  take him away they put  handcuffs on me i got scared he was  locked up and released on bail a few  hours later in a criminal complaint  chen was accused of failing to disclose  information about his ongoing  affiliations with the people's republic  of china as required on a 2017 energy  department grant application at a press  conference then u.s attorney andrew ling  made plain the stakes of the case this  was not just about greed  but about loyalty to china there's no  facts in what they said  so let me just ask you for the record  have you ever  been a spy for china absolutely no  spying i'm a u.s citizen and  so the answer is just absolutely no but  the world-renowned mechanical engineer  faced up to 20 years in prison  one of more than two dozen researchers  charged under the justice department's  china initiative  most of them ethnic chinese we are here  today to say enough  is enough former doj officials tell cbs  news the prosecutions were meant to send  a message to deter the academic  community from hiding research ties that  china could exploit i felt it was an  attack on all chinese americans in  america particularly in academia mit  president rafael reif said the news of  chen's arrest came as a massive jolt i  had to say i need to know more not  because i didn't trust my colleague but  the department of justice we don't  expect them to make a mistake reif  immediately spotted their first mistake  prosecutors accused chen of personally  pocketing 19 million dollars from an mit  collaboration with a chinese university  in shenzhen i know  that that is not true because i know the  money came to mit for the research the  collaboration was even advertised on  mit's website  reif decided the school would pay chen's  legal fees he was accusing of doing  something wrong for doing his job  so i had to come to his help the justice  department denies bias in its china  initiative cases we never  investigate or prosecute based on  ethnic identity but a year after it was  filed doj's case against gong chen fell  apart documents reviewed by cbs news  revealed an energy department official  told doj in early january  they'd got it wrong he never hid  anything he did from anybody within  weeks prosecutors called chen's attorney  robert fisher to tell him they had  dismissed the indictment and his client  was a free man how often have you seen a  full and outright dismissal like this  it's very rare and what does it indicate  it indicates a major mistake was made  and yet we found doj has dropped eight  cases against ethnic chinese researchers  charged under the china initiative  president reiff says the cases have had  a chilling effect we do have a problem  with china all i'm saying is just going  to universities and look for chinese  americans and doubting their loyalty to  this nation is not the right approach  use a scalpel not a hammer absolutely  after being placed on leave chen has  returned to teaching at mit this  semester still haunted by his arrest we  try very hard to go back to normal but  also i know i will never be the same  country again  andrew lelling the u.s attorney who  questioned chen's loyalty is now in  private practice and declined our  request for an interview the justice  department also declined an interview  but after we reached out the department  told cbs news it had concluded an  internal review of the china initiative  and later today  is expected to announce changes to the  program six cases are still pending  against researchers three of whom  are american citizens changes we hope  are improvements i i would be curious to  know i know that mr chen has a piece of  paper that says the charges have been  dropped did he get an apology not only  didn't he get an apology just the  opposite one of the prosecutors involved  in the case told us he has no regrets  about the prosecution because it  achieved a policy aim of  general deterrence meaning anybody  thinking about doing something similar  will now think twice except for one  almost  collateral damage yeah exactly on the  back of an innocent man  jim thank you

